{{nihongo|'''Honda Shigemasa'''|本多重正|Honda Shigemasa}} (22 August 1605 – 15 September 1671) was the fourth [[shogun]] of the Honda Shogunate in Mitoyagi (modern-day [[Okaiken]]). As the eldest son of the second shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]], and the grandson of the shogunate's founder, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]], Shigemasa inherited a military government that had been stabilized by his uncle, the third shogun [[Honda Shigetoki]]. 

Shigemasa is widely remembered as the most ruthless and authoritarian ruler in Sanese history. Abandoning the diplomatic clemency of his uncle, Shigemasa ruled through absolute terror, executing potential rivals and completely stripping the daimyō class of their remaining autonomy. His reign is defined by the implementation of draconian nationwide cadastral surveys, the brutal suppression of all non-Buddhist religions, and the enactment of the Great Seclusion laws. These policies effectively sealed Mitoyagi's fate, locking the island nation into a deeply stagnant but unbroken two-hundred-year period of isolationist peace known as the Pax Honda.

==Early Life and the Shadow of Kinzō==
Shigemasa was born in 1605 in the new capital of [[Okami]], the eldest son of Honda Shigetomo and his primary wife, Lady Saigō. Through his paternal grandmother, [[Hatsu-no-kata]], Shigemasa was the great-grandson of the legendary naval commander [[Anjō Motonobu]]. 

During his youth, Shigemasa was groomed for supreme command, mirroring the harsh military education his father had received. However, his life was violently upended in 1625 during the [[Kinzō Incident]]. When Shigemasa was just twenty years old, a coalition of disgruntled vassals assassinated his grandfather, the Retired Emperor Shirakawa. Days later, his father Shigetomo was mortally wounded during a grief-fueled retaliatory siege at [[Ichijoudani]]. 

To prevent a nationwide civil war, the Imperial Court under [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]] bypassed the young Shigemasa and installed his uncle, Honda Shigetoki, as the third Shogun. Shigetoki famously issued the Edict of Clemency, pardoning the rebellious [[Ii clan]] to secure peace. Historical records indicate that Shigemasa deeply resented his uncle's pragmatic mercy, viewing the Great Reconciliation as a profound insult to his father's memory. He spent the twenty-six years of his uncle's rule quietly building a network of fiercely loyal, hardline military retainers within Okami Castle, biding his time.

==Ascension and the Purges==
Upon the peaceful death of Honda Shigetoki on 12 November 1651, the 46-year-old Shigemasa finally ascended as the fourth Shogun. Almost immediately, he moved to consolidate absolute power and dismantle the conciliatory political structure his uncle had built. 

Shigemasa firmly believed that clemency bred weakness and that the only way to prevent another Kinzō Incident was through overwhelming fear. Within the first two years of his reign, he instigated the Keian Purges. Utilizing the shogunate's secret police (the metsuke), Shigemasa manufactured charges of treason against dozens of powerful tozama (outsider) and fudai (insider) daimyos who had shown independent ambitions during Shigetoki's lenient reign. Entire clans were summarily stripped of their lands (kaieki) or forced to commit seppuku. 

To ensure the physical subjugation of the lords, Shigemasa institutionalized the sankin-kōtai (alternate attendance) system. All daimyos were legally required to spend alternating years living in Okami, while their wives and heirs were kept permanently in the capital as hostages of the shogunate. Any clan that dared usurp power or resist the mandates was met with immediate, merciless military annihilation.

==Cadastral Surveys and Economic Control==
While his father Shigetomo had initiated the early Kinzō reforms to nationalize land surveying, it was Shigemasa who weaponized the system. In 1654, he ordered the commencement of the Great Shōō Cadastral Surveys. 

Unlike previous surveys which relied partially on the self-reporting of local lords, Shigemasa dispatched thousands of heavily armed shogunate officials to physically measure every rice paddy, field, and fishery across the Sanese islands. The surveys were notoriously exacting and unforgiving; peasants and local administrators caught hiding arable land from the inspectors were frequently executed on the spot. 

By standardizing the kokudaka (rice yield) system with terrifying precision, Shigemasa maximized the tax revenue flowing directly into Okami. This absolute economic stranglehold impoverished the regional daimyos, ensuring they could never afford to fund private armies or purchase foreign firearms to challenge the central government.

==Religious Suppression==
Shigemasa viewed any ideology that superseded absolute loyalty to the Shogun as a direct threat to the state. Building upon his grandfather's initial expulsion edicts, Shigemasa launched a systematic and bloody campaign to eradicate all foreign and non-conformist faiths.

He specifically targeted Christianity, which had survived underground in regions like [[Ishijima]] following the edicts of 1614. Shigemasa escalated the use of fumi-e (the practice of trampling on holy images) from a sporadic test into a mandatory annual requirement for every citizen. Those who refused were subjected to horrific public tortures, including crucifixion and boiling at the hot springs of Mount Unzen. 

To enforce ideological homogeneity, Shigemasa legally mandated the Danka system. Every citizen of Mitoyagi, from the highest samurai to the lowest peasant, was required to register with a local Jōdo-shū Buddhist temple. The Buddhist clergy were effectively transformed into an arm of the shogunate's intelligence network, responsible for monitoring the populace and reporting any signs of religious dissent or hidden Kirishitan communities. By tying citizenship and social standing strictly to Buddhist temple registration, Shigemasa successfully eradicated competing religious influence.

==The Closed Country Policy (Kaikin)==
Shigemasa's most enduring legacy was his decision to sever Mitoyagi from the rest of the world. Fearing that foreign powers would arm domestic rebels or dilute Sanese culture, he enacted the Kaikin (Maritime Prohibitions) edicts between 1655 and 1659.

Under Shigemasa's absolute decree, it became a capital offense for any Sanese citizen to leave the country. Furthermore, any Sanese who had previously lived abroad was forbidden from returning, effectively stranding thousands of merchants and sailors. The construction of ocean-going vessels with keels was strictly outlawed, relegating the once-proud Anjō-Murakami Suigun to a coastal defense and policing force. 

Foreign trade was violently curtailed. Volisanian and Zong merchants were banned from all mainland ports. A singular, heavily guarded artificial island was constructed off the coast of [[Yokosuka, Miyagi|Yokosuka]], where a severely limited number of foreign vessels were permitted to dock once a year under the watchful eyes of the shogunate's military. This policy sealed Mitoyagi into a technological and cultural time capsule, guaranteeing the absolute survival of the Honda Shogunate for over two centuries, but severely stunting the nation's global development.

==Death and Legacy==
Shigemasa ruled with an iron fist until his death from stomach cancer on 15 September 1671. He was succeeded by his son, [[Honda Shigenori]], who maintained the strict legal codes and isolationist policies established by his father. 

History views Honda Shigemasa as a highly polarizing figure. While modern democratic scholars often condemn him as a paranoid tyrant who plunged Okaiken into a dark age of oppressive censorship, traditionalist historians argue that his ruthless centralization was a grim necessity. By breaking the spirit of the warlords and sealing the borders, Shigemasa engineered the Pax Honda—a two-hundred-year span devoid of major warfare, allowing internal culture, literature, and domestic agriculture to flourish without the constant threat of the Sengoku bloodshed his ancestors had endured.

==In Popular Culture==
Due to his reputation as a tyrant, Shigemasa is almost exclusively portrayed as an antagonist or a terrifying, Machiavellian anti-hero in Sanese media. In the iconic Taisen Drama ''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]'' (1983), the final episodes depict a young Shigemasa seething with quiet rage at his uncle's clemency. He is the central figure in the 2024 series ''[[Power to Live]]'', which controversially explores the psychological toll of enforcing the closed-country policies and the brutal purges required to maintain total peace.

